Monkey patching is a programming technique where you modify or extend code at runtime,
typically by changing or adding
attributes (methods, variables) to classes or modules without altering the original source code.

Monkey patching = dynamically changing code behavior at runtime without touching the original implementation.

Common uses
Fixing bugs in third-party libraries temporarily
Adding features to existing code without modifying it
Testing (e.g., mocking behavior)

Downsides
Can make code harder to understand and debug
Unexpected side effects if other parts rely on original behavior
Considered risky in large or production systems